# You design and build LEGO Technic models one step at a time, in a build language.
#
# For each step you write:
# ADD name partnumber color
# CONNECT part.point -> part.point LOCKED|TURNS|STIFF   (points: hole1.., axlehole1.., pin1.., axle1.., clip1..; LOCKED = cross in cross, turn together; TURNS = free; STIFF = friction pin)
# Optional: MESH a.teeth b.teeth / RATIO a b x/y / GROUP g (opens a side build) / END (closes it) / ATTACH g (joins a finished side build into the main model)
# WHY one plain sentence: what this step builds and what it is for.
# A point ending in ~ is an estimate; ? means not known.
#
# Rules: only use real points of each part; a round pin never goes in a cross hole; a shaft that must turn needs round holes; a worm needs stops on both sides; gears must sit at the right distance.
STEP 1
ADD bracket-L 6536 lightgray
ADD pin-blue-A 43093 blue
ADD pin-red-A 11214 red
CONNECT pin-blue-A.axle1 -> bracket-L.axlehole1 LOCKED
CONNECT pin-red-A.pin1 -> bracket-L.hole1~ STIFF
DOES bracket-L HOLDS pin-blue-A
DOES bracket-L HOLDS pin-red-A
PATTERN peg_in_elbow_bracket
WHY The elbow bracket has only one round hole and one cross hole; a friction peg in each turns those into two usable mounting studs at right angles, so the beam and the frame's bottom corner can both attach here later.

STEP 2
ADD bracket-R 6536 lightgray
ADD peg-black-B 43093 black
ADD fork-R 32291 lightgray
CONNECT peg-black-B.axle1 -> bracket-R.axlehole1 LOCKED
DOES bracket-R HOLDS peg-black-B
PATTERN peg_in_elbow_bracket
WHY A friction peg with a stop on both ends bridges the elbow bracket's cross hole now and the beam's round hole in step 3, same trick as pin-blue-A on the left.

STEP 3
ADD beam-C 32524 lightgray
CONNECT pin-blue-A.pin1 -> beam-C.hole1 STIFF
CONNECT peg-black-B.pin1 -> beam-C.hole7 STIFF
DOES beam-C HOLDS pin-blue-A
DOES beam-C HOLDS peg-black-B
PATTERN close_the_u
WHY A single 7-hole beam is long enough to reach both corners, with 5 spare holes in the middle for the shift mechanism (steps 4-6).

STEP 4
ADD pin-blue-D 43093 blue
ADD pin-blue-E 43093 blue
ADD pin-blue-F 43093 blue
CONNECT pin-blue-D.pin1 -> beam-C.hole3 STIFF
CONNECT pin-blue-E.pin1 -> beam-C.hole4 STIFF
CONNECT pin-blue-F.pin1 -> beam-C.hole5 STIFF
DOES beam-C HOLDS pin-blue-D
DOES beam-C HOLDS pin-blue-E
DOES beam-C HOLDS pin-blue-F
PATTERN three_pegs_in_a_row
WHY Same friction peg as pin-blue-A: a cross-hole axle end hanging free is exactly what a bracket's cross hole needs next.

STEP 5
ADD bracket-G 6536 lightgray
ADD bracket-H 6536 lightgray
CONNECT pin-blue-D.axle1 -> bracket-G.axlehole1 LOCKED
CONNECT pin-blue-F.axle1 -> bracket-H.axlehole1 LOCKED
DOES pin-blue-D CARRIES bracket-G
DOES pin-blue-F CARRIES bracket-H
PATTERN mirror_the_side
WHY Locking a bracket onto each axle end fixes it so it can't spin loose; leaving the middle peg bare keeps it free for the shift housing's cross hole next.

STEP 6
ADD fork-J 32172 red
ADD knob-I 6628 lightgray
CONNECT pin-blue-E.axle1 -> fork-J.axlehole1 LOCKED
CONNECT knob-I.pin1 -> fork-J.hole1 STIFF
DOES pin-blue-E CARRIES fork-J
DOES fork-J HOLDS knob-I
PATTERN lever_on_a_slider
WHY fork-J's slot is sized to sit in the groove of the sliding clutch sleeve built in step 9, without turning with the shaft itself.

STEP 7
ADD bush-K 3713 lightgray
ADD gear-gray-L 4019 lightgray
ADD gear-tan-M 32269 tan
ADD axle-N 3707 black
CONNECT axle-N.axle1 -> bush-K.axlehole1 LOCKED
CONNECT axle-N.axle3 -> gear-gray-L.axlehole1 LOCKED
CONNECT axle-N.axle7 -> gear-tan-M.axlehole1 LOCKED
CONNECT axle-N.axle8 -> fork-R.hole1~ TURNS
DOES axle-N CARRIES bush-K
DOES axle-N CARRIES gear-gray-L
DOES axle-N CARRIES gear-tan-M
DOES axle-N SPINS_ON fork-R
PATTERN shaft_with_stops
WHY gear-gray-L and gear-tan-M are locked to the shaft (cross-hole gears) so they always turn with it; bush-K keeps the shaft from sliding left; fork-R's round hole lets the shaft spin free on the right.

STEP 8
ADD axle-brown-O 3705 brown
ADD gear-red-P 18946 red
CONNECT axle-brown-O.axle2 -> gear-red-P.hole1 TURNS
DOES gear-red-P SPINS_ON axle-brown-O
PATTERN stub_shaft_extension
WHY gear-red-P has clutch (dog-tooth) faces on both sides and a plain round hole, so by design it never locks to a shaft on its own -- only the sliding clutch sleeve from step 9 can lock it in.

STEP 9
ADD sleeve-wht-Q 6538b white
ADD sleeve-blk-S 6538b black
CONNECT axle-N.axle5 -> sleeve-wht-Q.axlehole1~ LOCKED
CONNECT axle-N.axle6 -> sleeve-blk-S.axlehole1~ LOCKED
DOES axle-N CARRIES sleeve-wht-Q
DOES axle-N CARRIES sleeve-blk-S
PATTERN sliding_dog_clutch
WHY A cross-hole connector locked to the shaft always turns with it (unlike gear-red-P's free hole), but can still slide along the shaft's length -- that sliding motion, guided by fork-J's slot, is what engages or disengages gear-red-P's clutch face.

STEP 10
ADD axle-input-T 3705 black
ADD gear-blue-U 35185 blue
CONNECT axle-input-T.axle1 -> bracket-G.hole1~ TURNS
CONNECT axle-input-T.axle2 -> gear-blue-U.hole1~ TURNS
MESH gear-blue-U.teeth gear-gray-L.teeth
DOES gear-blue-U DRIVES gear-gray-L
RATIO gear-blue-U gear-gray-L 20/16
PATTERN input_gear_mesh
WHY A double-bevel gear meshes cleanly with gear-gray-L's plain teeth at this close spacing; its own clutch faces aren't used here, just its teeth.

STEP 11
ADD ext-V 6538b white
ADD ext-W 6538b white
CONNECT axle-input-T.axle3 -> ext-V.axlehole1~ LOCKED
CONNECT axle-N.axle8 -> ext-W.axlehole1~ LOCKED
DOES axle-input-T CARRIES ext-V
DOES axle-N CARRIES ext-W
PATTERN axle_extension
WHY A ridged axle connector gives a longer, grippable stub with a cross hole on its far end, so the shaft can reach past the frame and still take another axle.
